# Testing

- Every new function containing business logic ships with unit tests in the same change. Bug fixes ship with a regression test that fails without the fix.
- Test behavior, not implementation. Assert on outputs and observable effects, never on private internals — tests should survive refactors.
- Cover the three cases for every unit: the happy path, edge cases (empty, null, boundary values, unicode, large inputs), and failure modes (invalid input, dependency errors).
- Test names describe the scenario and expectation: `returns 404 when order does not exist`, not `test order 2`.
- Follow Arrange–Act–Assert structure with one logical assertion focus per test. If a test needs "and" in its name, split it.
- Mock external dependencies (network, DB, clock, randomness) at the boundary; never mock the code under test. Prefer test doubles injected via parameters over module patching.
- Tests must be deterministic and independent: no shared mutable state, no reliance on execution order, no real timers or network.
- Keep tests fast. If a test needs real infrastructure, mark it as an integration test and keep it out of the default unit suite.
